  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSurface high pressure was centered across western PA early this\nmorning. The high will continue to move east through Sunday,\nmaintaining dry weather across the region. Highs should be near\nseasonable levels today, with low humidity. Rising 500mb\nheights, and a veering flow from east to south, should result \nin warmer temperatures and slightly higher humidity on Sunday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA ridge is progged to build eastward from the central CONUS from\nlate Sunday into the middle of next week. Models differ on the\nplacement and northern extent of the ridge, especially early in\nthe week. Individual shortwaves are expected to track across \nthe northern periphery of the ridge, and the strength/placement\nof the ridge will have an impact on how far south this activity\nreaches. \n\nIn general, the first shortwave could spread shower and\nthunderstorm chances to areas near and north of I-80 Sunday\nnight into Monday. The ridge is expected to continue advancing\neastward, strengthening across the Upper Ohio Valley region \nlater Monday into Tuesday. If this scenario occur, it would \nlikely suppress most of the convection across our area. The \n500mb heights should peak across the Upper Ohio Valley region \nlater Tuesday through part of Wednesday, before the ridge begins\nto break down. In general, expect shower and thunderstorm \nchances to increase as the ridge breaks down, and as a shortwave\ntrough approaches late Wednesday into Thursday. The latest ML \nguidance indicates some potential for severe storms especially \nWednesday into Thursday, though this will be highly dependent on\nthe placement of the ridge, timing of its breakdown, and timing\nof the shortwave.\n\nRising 500mb heights and SW flow should result in warming\ntemperatures through mid week, with highs around 90 Tuesday and\nWednesday for most locations outside of the higher terrain. This\nwill also depend on the placement and strength of the ridge. The\nheat and humidity could result in heat index values in the mid\nto upper 90s both of those days. Temperatures should begin to \nmoderate by late week.",
